  src/services/db/connection.ts — transactions run on a dedicated SQLite
  connection instead of the plugin's connection pool.
- src/services/calendar/davFetch.ts (added),
  src/services/calendar/autoDiscovery.ts,
  src/services/calendar/caldavProvider.ts — CalDAV requests go through the Rust
  HTTP client, and discovery also probes the configured mail server host.
- src/services/db/accounts.ts, src/components/accounts/AddCalDavAccount.tsx —
  CalDAV settings attach to an existing account for the same address.
- src/services/calendar/recurrence.ts (added),
  src/services/calendar/timezone.ts (added),
  src/services/calendar/occurrences.ts (added),
  src/services/calendar/icalHelper.ts, src/services/calendar/types.ts,
  src/services/db/calendarEvents.ts, src/services/db/migrations.ts,
  src/components/calendar/ — recurring CalDAV events are expanded into their
  individual instances on the client. A CalDAV server returns the series master
  rather than expanded instances, so a recurring event previously appeared only
  once. Recurrence is evaluated in the time zone of the series' DTSTART, and the
  iCalendar parser was made component-aware so a VTIMEZONE's own rules are no
  longer read as the event's.
- src/services/calendar/icalEdit.ts (added),
  src/services/calendar/caldavProvider.ts,
  src/components/calendar/EventDetailModal.tsx — edits patch the stored
  calendar object instead of regenerating the VEVENT, which previously
  discarded the recurrence rule, time zone, alarms and per-instance overrides.
  Single occurrences can be changed or removed separately from their series.
- src/services/calendar/errors.ts (added),
  src/services/calendar/caldavProvider.ts, src/services/calendar/icalEdit.ts,
  src/services/calendar/recurrence.ts, src/services/calendar/types.ts,
  src/services/calendar/googleCalendarProvider.ts,
  src/components/calendar/EventDetailModal.tsx — corrections to the CalDAV
  write paths. If-Match is now taken from the calendar object's etag rather
  than passed as a headers parameter, which tsdav merges over the
  authorization header so every PUT and DELETE was refused with 401. A refused
  write raises an error instead of being reported as saved, with 412 shown as
  a conflict. Splitting a series stores the remainder before bounding the
  original and takes it back if that fails, and rebases the rule's COUNT so
  the two halves add up to the original series. UNTIL is written with the
  value type DTSTART uses, and cutting a series before its first instance
  deletes the object instead of leaving a series that has no instances.
- src/services/calendar/googleCalendarProvider.ts — an all-day event from
  Google Calendar now ends at midnight of the date the API reports as its end
  rather than at 23:59:59 of it. That date is documented as the exclusive end,
  and every calendar view buckets by half-open overlap, so a one-day event was
  also drawn on the following day.
- src/services/calendar/recurrenceForm.ts (added),
  src/components/calendar/RecurrenceField.tsx (added),
  src/services/calendar/icalHelper.ts, src/services/calendar/icalEdit.ts,
  src/services/calendar/recurrence.ts, src/services/calendar/timezone.ts,
  src/services/calendar/types.ts, src/services/calendar/caldavProvider.ts,
  src/services/calendar/googleCalendarProvider.ts,
  src/components/calendar/CalendarPage.tsx,
  src/components/calendar/EventCreateModal.tsx,
  src/components/calendar/EventDetailModal.tsx — repetition can be set up and
  changed in the dialogs rather than only read. A generated VEVENT now carries
  an RRULE, and an existing rule can be replaced or removed on the series
  master; UNTIL is written with the value type the series' DTSTART uses. A rule
  the control cannot state — an ordinal weekday, a BYSETPOS — is shown but left
  untouched instead of being silently downgraded, and a control nobody touched
  sends nothing at all, so editing a title never rewrites the repetition.
  Changing the rule drops the exceptions and per-instance overrides the old one
  anchored, which the expander would otherwise render as occurrences the series
  no longer has.
- src/components/calendar/EventCreateModal.tsx,
  src/components/calendar/CalendarPage.tsx — an event can be marked as lasting
  all day when it is created. The dialog then asks for two dates instead of two
  times, and the day picked as the end is carried forward by one before it is
  sent, because DTEND (RFC 5545 §3.6.1) and the Google API's end.date both name
  the day after the last one the event covers. An end a moved start would leave
  behind follows it along, so the range never runs backwards.
- src/services/calendar/allDay.ts (added),
  src/components/calendar/EventDetailModal.tsx,
  src/components/calendar/EventCreateModal.tsx,
  src/services/calendar/caldavProvider.ts — the edit dialog knows about all-day
  events. It asks for two dates instead of two times, shows the day the event
  ends on rather than the exclusive boundary after it, and states the all-day
  flag on every save: without it the Google provider wrote start.dateTime, so
  saving any change at all turned an all-day event into a timed one. Whether an
  event lasts all day stays as it was created — a stored CalDAV object fixes the
  value type of every date it carries — and the switch is shown disabled rather
  than offering a change that could not be carried out.
- src/components/calendar/CalendarToolbar.tsx — the previous/today/next buttons
  are placed before the period title rather than after it, so their position no
  longer shifts with the length of the month or date being shown, and they carry
  accessible labels. The bar wraps when the calendar is narrow instead of
  drawing the view switcher over the navigation.
- src/components/calendar/CalendarPage.tsx — the box holding the day, week and
  month views is a flex container, so the views can size themselves to the
  window. As a plain block it left them at their content height: a month
  needing six week rows ran past the bottom of the window while a five-row
  month stopped short of it.
- src/services/dav/contentLine.ts (added), src/services/calendar/icalHelper.ts —
  the line format iCalendar and vCard share (folding, parameter quoting, text
  escaping) was extracted so both parsers use one implementation. Parameters
  that repeat now accumulate instead of the last one winning, and a bare
  parameter is read as a type, as vCard 2.1 writers emit it.
- src/services/contacts/ (added), src/services/db/addressBooks.ts (added),
  src/services/db/contacts.ts, src/services/db/accounts.ts,
  src/services/db/migrations.ts, src/services/gmail/syncManager.ts,
  src/components/contacts/ (added),
  src/components/accounts/AddCardDavAccount.tsx (added),
  src/components/settings/CardDavSettings.tsx (added) — address books are
  synced over CardDAV. The contacts table previously held only addresses
  harvested from mail headers and required each row to carry exactly one, which
  a vCard breaks in both directions: it may list several addresses or none. The
  table now records where a row came from, and uniqueness holds per origin
  rather than globally. A synced card owns the identity of its addresses while
  the local row keeps what only this app knows — how often and how recently
  mail was exchanged — and a mail-derived row is folded into the card that
  claims its address, carrying any note the user wrote onto the server first.
  Edits patch the stored vCard rather than regenerating it, in the card's own
  version, so photos, custom properties and another client's extensions
  survive a save. CardDAV settings attach to an existing account for the same
  address, as the CalDAV ones do.
- src/components/email/EmailRenderer.tsx, public/emailFrame.js (added) — the
  email body frame reports link clicks and its height over postMessage instead
  of relying on a listener the app attaches to the frame document, which WebKit
  never invokes on a sandboxed frame. The frame now runs on an opaque origin.
- src/components/layout/Sidebar.tsx — each navigation entry states which group
  it belongs to, mailboxes or the separate areas of the app, and the sidebar
  draws a divider wherever the group changes. Tasks, Calendar and Attachments
  therefore read as their own block instead of continuing the list of
  mailboxes. The divider follows the grouping rather than a fixed position,
  because the order of the entries can be rearranged in Settings.
- src/utils/linkify.ts (added), src/components/email/EmailRenderer.tsx — URLs in
  a plain-text message body are turned into anchors instead of being rendered as
  inert text.
- src/components/email/MessageItem.tsx,
  src/components/email/EmailRenderer.tsx,
  src/components/email/PhishingBanner.tsx,
  src/services/phishing/phishingScanner.ts, src/utils/phishingDetector.ts — the
  phishing link scan is now invoked when a message body is shown. A warning
  banner appears above the body, and a link the scan flagged is confirmed
  before it opens. The scan cache holds link scores only, so the sensitivity
  setting applies to messages that were scanned earlier as well.
- src/services/quickSteps/executor.ts — a quick step's snooze action writes its
  wake-up time as a Unix timestamp in seconds. The duration is configured in
  milliseconds and was added to Date.now() unconverted, so threads.snooze_until
  held a value roughly a thousand times too large and a thread snoozed by a
  quick step never came back.
- src/services/db/migrations.ts, src/services/db/migrations.test.ts — a
  follow-up reminder can be saved. Its upsert targets ON CONFLICT(account_id,
  thread_id) while the schema indexed that pair non-uniquely, and SQLite
  rejects a conflict target that matches no unique constraint at prepare time,
  so every attempt to set a reminder failed. The index is now unique, and the
  conflict targets of all db modules are checked against the schema.
- src/services/search/ftsQuery.ts (added), src/services/db/search.ts,
  src/services/search/searchQueryBuilder.ts,
  src/components/search/SearchBar.tsx — free text is quoted into FTS5 phrases
  before it reaches MATCH. FTS5 parses that argument as a query expression, so
  an address, a hyphen or an apostrophe in an ordinary search raised a syntax
  error; the search box swallowed it and cleared the thread filter, and a
  cleared filter means "no search running", so the unfiltered mailbox was shown
  as though it were the result. Each term is quoted separately, which keeps the
  implicit AND that an unquoted query already had. A search that does fail now
  reports nothing found instead of everything.
- src/components/composer/Composer.tsx, src/stores/composerStore.ts — the
  composer's editor is filled with the body it was opened with. App and
  ThreadWindow mount the composer unconditionally, so TipTap was created once
  at startup with the store still empty and its content option never described
  the mail being written: a reply's quote, a forwarded message and a draft
  loaded from the database all opened into an empty editor, and the text of
  the previous mail was still in it. The store counts compose sessions so an
  open on top of an open is recognised as well.
- src-tauri/src/smtp/client.rs — the Bcc header is removed before the message
  is handed to DATA. The composer writes a real Bcc field into the MIME source
  so the SMTP envelope can be built from it, and lettre's send_raw passes the
  bytes on unchanged, so on the IMAP/SMTP path every recipient was told who had
  been blind copied. RFC 5322 section 3.6.3 has the field removed before
  transmission; the envelope is still taken from the message first, because the
  blind recipients are recorded nowhere else. The Gmail API path is untouched,
  as Google strips the field server side, and so is the copy appended to the
  Sent folder, where the sender's own record of the field belongs.
- src-tauri/src/smtp/client.rs — the security setting is matched exactly:
  "tls", "starttls" and "none" build a transport, any other value is rejected
  with an error. A catch-all arm used to treat every unrecognised value as
  "none", so a typo or a future setting sent the credentials unencrypted.
- src/components/composer/Composer.tsx,
  src/components/composer/UndoSendToast.tsx, src/stores/composerStore.ts —
  cancelling a send through the undo toast gives the mail back and leaves
  sending working. The guard that stops a double submit was released only by
  the timer that actually sends, so cancelling left it set for the rest of the
  session and every later send returned silently; writing a second mail inside
  the five-second window hit the same guard. It now covers only the scheduling
  itself. The composed mail is kept for the length of the window, because the
  composer is closed and the store emptied as soon as the send is scheduled,
  and undo previously discarded the text along with the send.
- src/services/imap/imapSync.ts — a delta sync describes a thread from every
  message it holds rather than only the ones just fetched. Delta sync fetches
  what is new and threads what it fetched, and the thread row and its labels
  were rewritten from that batch alone: a reply into a stored conversation
  counted it down to one message, cleared a star sitting on a message that was
  not re-fetched, replaced the subject with the reply's, and deleted every
  label the user had applied. The aggregate is now read back from the stored
  messages and the labels are merged instead of replaced. The initial sync
  path is unchanged, since there the batch is the whole thread.
- src/services/imap/imapSync.ts — the initial sync no longer records a folder
  as synced past a chunk it failed to fetch. A chunk is abandoned after one
  retry and the run carries on, but last_uid was written from the highest UID
  that came back, which comes from a later chunk. Since delta sync asks only
  for UIDs above that watermark, the abandoned messages were never fetched
  again by any sync.
- src/services/imap/imapSync.ts, src/services/db/messages.ts,
  src/services/db/threads.ts — a folder whose UIDVALIDITY changed is cleared
  before it is fetched again. A message's local id is built from its UID, so
  when the server withdraws the meaning of those UIDs the resync writes every
  message under a new id and the rows from before were left beside them: the
  whole folder appeared twice and no later sync could reach the old copy.
  Threads left without messages are removed with them.
- src-tauri/src/imap/client.rs — deleting or moving messages no longer issues
  a bare EXPUNGE. That command removes every \Deleted message in the mailbox
  (RFC 3501 section 6.4.3), so deleting two messages of this app's own could
  destroy mail another client had flagged and deliberately left in place.
  Where the server offers UIDPLUS the removal is limited to the messages
  concerned with UID EXPUNGE (RFC 4315 section 2.1); where it does not, the
  \Deleted flag is set and nothing is expunged.
- src-tauri/src/imap/client.rs — the raw IMAP fallbacks quote the arguments of
  LOGIN and SELECT. A password or folder name containing a double quote or a
  backslash was interpolated into the command unescaped and broke it apart
  (RFC 3501 section 4.3); a value containing a line break would have ended the
  command line altogether and is now refused rather than sent.
- src/services/snooze/scheduledSendManager.ts — a scheduled mail is sent
  through the account's own provider rather than through a Gmail client. On an
  IMAP account there is none, so the send threw, the error did not look
  transient, and the row was marked failed: the mail was dropped at its
  scheduled time without being sent and without a word to the user.
- src/services/dav/contentLine.ts — the escapes of a TEXT value are read in a
  single pass. The rule for "\n" ran before the one for "\\" and consumed the
  second backslash of an escaped pair, so an escaped backslash followed by an
  "n" was read as a line break: a Windows path in a NOTE or DESCRIPTION came
  back cut in half and was written to the server in that state on the next
  save.
- src/services/calendar/recurrence.ts — a yearly series starting on 29
  February produces no instance in a common year instead of one on 1 March.
  The expansion returned the start's month and day for every year without
  checking that the date exists, and the conversion rolled it forward. RFC 5545
  §3.3.10 requires an invalid date to be ignored, which the monthly expansion
  already did.
- src/services/calendar/recurrenceForm.ts — a series bounded by an end date
  runs to the end of that day in the zone the day belongs to. A DTSTART
  written as a UTC stamp, which is what this app generates for every event it
  creates, was taken literally and put UNTIL at 23:59:59 UTC, so for anyone
  west of UTC an evening instance on the chosen last day fell after the bound
  and disappeared from the series. A series carrying a TZID is bounded in that
  zone as before.
- src/services/phishing/phishingScanner.ts,
  src/components/email/MessageItem.tsx — the phishing scan covers a message
  with no HTML part. It was handed body_html alone, which is null for such a
  message, and returned no links at all — while the renderer linkifies the
  plain-text body and shows those links as anchors, so a plain-text phishing
  mail raised no banner and its links opened without confirmation. The plain
  text is now linkified with the same function the renderer uses, so exactly
  the links that become clickable are the ones scored. Cache entries carry a
  scanner version, so results recorded before this are rescanned rather than
  standing for good.
- src-tauri/src/oauth.rs — the OAuth callback server binds the port the
  redirect URI names and no other. It used to fall back to the next three
  ports when the first was taken, while the redirect URI is built from a fixed
  port on the TypeScript side: the browser delivered the code to a port nothing
  was listening on and the sign-in hung until its five-minute timeout. Starting
  a sign-in now also ends one that was abandoned earlier, which is what had
  been holding the port for those five minutes.
- src/components/email/ContactSidebar.tsx — a note written on a contact synced
  from an address book is saved to the card on the server rather than to the
  local row alone. The server owns a synced card's identity and contactSync
  writes what it holds over the local row, so the note survived only until the
  next sync. The sidebar now saves through the same path the contact editor
  uses, which knows whether a contact has a server to reach; for a synced card
  the save happens when the field is left, so two writes cannot race each
  other's etag.
- src-tauri/tauri.conf.json, src/utils/imageBlocker.ts — a remote image the
  user has chosen to load actually reaches the message frame. That frame is a
  srcdoc iframe and inherits the application's content security policy, whose
  img-src named only Gravatar and Google's avatar hosts: every other remote
  image was refused by the policy no matter what the user asked for, which made
  the "Load images" button and the image_allowlist table inert. Whether an image
  loads is decided by stripRemoteImages() and that table, so the policy no
  longer restates the decision and permits any https source. Cleartext http
  images stay refused. The blocker in turn now treats a protocol-relative
  "//host/path" as remote, which it had ignored: with the policy narrow such an
  image could not load anyway, and with it widened the frame's base URL would
  have decided.
- src/services/db/migrations.ts — each migration runs in a transaction on the
  dedicated SQLite connection, and callers that start together share one run.
  runMigrations() still sent BEGIN, the statements and COMMIT through the
  plugin's pooled handle, the one place the move to a dedicated connection had
  left out. That holds only while a single pooled connection is in use. In a
  development build React's StrictMode runs the startup effect twice, the two
  runs spread over several connections, and a fresh database ended in one of
  three states: a connection left holding the write lock, so that every later
  write failed with SQLITE_BUSY and the first account could not be saved; one
  run aborted by that lock; or both aborted with part of the schema missing.
  Sharing the run is needed as well as moving it, because a second run repeats
  the migrations the first has committed and fails on the first statement that
  is not idempotent.
- src/services/emailActions.ts, src/services/db/messages.ts — an action on a
  thread now names the messages it applies to. Every caller acts on a thread and
  passed an empty list of message IDs; Gmail is addressed per thread and did not
  care, but IMAP has no threads, so the provider grouped an empty list by folder
  and archiving, deleting, starring, marking read, reporting spam and moving to a
  folder never reached the server. The messages are resolved from the local
  database before the thread is changed there, because a permanent delete drops
  the thread and its messages cascade with it, and the resolved list is stored on
  a queued operation so a replay still has it once those rows are gone.
- src/services/imap/imapSync.ts — a delta sync records a folder's last_uid
  only after the messages it fetched are stored. It used to write it first, so
  a failure while storing (a locked database, the app closing) left the
  watermark above mail that was never saved and the next delta never asked for
  it again. A message whose thread is skipped for a pending local operation now
  holds last_uid just below its UID, so it is fetched again rather than lost.
  The same goes for a UID the server listed and the fetch left out (the Rust
  side drops a message it cannot read with only a log line): it holds last_uid
  for up to two further syncs and is then given up on, so one message the
  server cannot serve does not pin the folder.
- src/styles/globals.css, src/App.tsx, src/ComposerWindow.tsx,
  src/ThreadWindow.tsx, src/stores/uiStore.ts, src/components/ — the interface
  is restyled as a monochrome design after Vercel's Geist system: Geist Sans and
  Geist Mono, hairline borders in place of glass panels and drop shadows, 6px
  radii, and a matching dark variant. The animated gradient background and the
  eight accent color presets are removed (src/constants/themes.ts is deleted and
  a stored color_theme setting is ignored). The "Reduce motion" setting now
  turns off all animations and transitions.
- Application identity renamed from Velo (com.velomail.app) to Maish
  (xyz.hochreiner.maish), including packaging metadata, the encryption key file
  name and the update endpoint.
- src-tauri/tauri.conf.json — updater artifacts and their signatures are produced
  on every build, so releases carry a signed latest.json.
- Upstream-specific material removed: the marketing site, release and packaging
  workflows, contribution and security policies, Velo's changelog, and Velo's
  logo and application icons, which are trademarks and not covered by the
  licence. Icons and logo are original to Maish.
- src/App.tsx, src/components/layout/EmailList.tsx,
  src/components/layout/Sidebar.tsx, src/stores/uiStore.ts,
  src/components/ui/SyncIndicator.tsx (added),
  src/components/email/SyncNowButton.tsx (added), src/utils/syncStatus.ts
  (added) — the full-width sync status bar is replaced by a small spinner in
  the sidebar's bottom bar that shows only while a sync runs, and a button next
  to the folder name starts a sync by hand. A failed sync leaves an error icon
  in place until that account syncs again, where the bar cleared it after eight
  seconds, and a failure is remembered per account so that the accounts synced
  after it do not wipe it out.
- src/services/threading/threadBuilder.ts, src/services/imap/imapSync.ts,
  src-tauri/src/imap/client.rs, src-tauri/src/imap/types.rs,
  src-tauri/Cargo.toml (`sha2` added) — messages that share an RFC Message-ID
  within one batch are treated as folder copies only if sender, date, subject
  and a SHA-256 content hash agree. Before, the last one silently replaced the
  others, so a message reusing another mail's Message-ID displaced it.
  Messages with differing content are now all kept in the thread, and only the
  first message with an ID decides where its container sits in the thread tree.
  The hash is computed in Rust (`ImapMessage.content_hash`) over the text part,
  the HTML part and every attachment's name, type, Content-ID, disposition and
  decoded bytes, but not its MIME section, which differs between folder copies.
  A folder copy whose Date header is missing or unreadable falls back to a
  per-folder date and is therefore shown twice rather than merged. When messages
  with one ID sit in different folders, the one synced first places the
  container, so a forged message in an earlier folder can still group a thread
  wrongly; nothing is lost.
- src/services/gmail/syncManager.ts, src/components/settings/SettingsPage.tsx —
  deleting an account removes it from the background sync, so it is no longer
  synced (and reported as failing) after it is gone; an empty sync run no longer
  leaves later runs queued behind a finished one. The sidebar's sync indicator
  keeps its space while idle so the bottom bar does not shift.
- src/services/unsubscribe/unsubscribeManager.ts — the one-click unsubscribe
  POST (RFC 8058) is sent only to https URLs and no longer follows redirects.
  Before, it accepted http URLs, including loopback addresses, and the HTTP
  plugin's scope was checked for the first URL only, so a redirect could carry
  the POST to a host in the local network. A redirect answer counts as a
  failure and the unsubscribe falls back to mailto or the browser.
- src-tauri/capabilities/default.json — the HTTP plugin no longer allows
  plaintext `http://` to any host. Requests of the plugin bypass the webview
  CSP, so the scope is now `https://*` plus `http://localhost:*` and
  `http://127.0.0.1:*`, which is what a local Ollama or LM Studio server needs.
- src-tauri/src/smtp/client.rs — an SMTP account with implicit TLS (port 465)
  and "accept invalid certificates" keeps implicit TLS. It was switched to
  STARTTLS-required, so the client waited for a plaintext greeting while the
  server expected a TLS handshake and sending failed.
- src/utils/crypto.ts, src/services/db/accounts.ts,
  src-tauri/src/credential_key.rs (added), src-tauri/src/lib.rs,
  src-tauri/Cargo.toml — the AES key that encrypts stored passwords and tokens
  is held in the OS keychain (macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, Secret
  Service on Linux) instead of in maish.key next to the database, where anyone
  able to read the data directory could decrypt every credential. An existing
  maish.key is moved into the keychain and deleted only after the stored
  credentials have been decrypted with the moved key; a new key is never
  generated while an old one exists or while encrypted credentials are stored.
  A credential that cannot be decrypted now raises an error naming the account
  and field, where the ciphertext used to be passed on as if it were the
  password. Adds the keyring crate (MIT OR Apache-2.0) as a dependency.
- src/services/ai/providers/claudeProvider.ts, geminiProvider.ts — the Claude and
  Gemini providers call their HTTP APIs with `fetch` instead of the vendors'
  SDKs, which are no longer dependencies. Requests are made once, with a
  60 second timeout: the Anthropic SDK retried twice on 408, 409, 429 and 5xx
  and waited up to ten minutes, so a single overloaded answer now fails the
  action instead of being retried. Errors read "Claude API error <status>" or
  "Gemini API error <status>". A Gemini reply cut off for SAFETY, RECITATION or
  LANGUAGE still raises an error, as it did with the SDK.
- src/components/settings/SettingsPage.tsx — the About panel links to this
  fork's own repository and nothing else. The website and contact rows were
  inherited from upstream with the name swapped in and pointed at addresses the
  project does not control; they were removed.
- src-tauri/src/commands.rs, src-tauri/src/lib.rs — the `imap_*` commands take
  their session from a per-account pool (`src-tauri/src/imap/pool.rs`, new
  file) instead of connecting and logging in for every command, and no longer
  send LOGOUT. A session whose command failed is dropped, never reused.
- src/services/gmail/syncManager.ts, src/components/settings/SettingsPage.tsx —
  "Full resync" and the per-account "Resync" button report what the sync did.
  They used to show "Done!" whatever happened, because a failed sync was
  swallowed; they now fail with the sync error, show "Queued..." while another
  sync holds the queue and the sync progress while their own runs. Sync failures
  are also written to the log file through a new `log_frontend` command
